---
/** 
 * hoodie-manufacturer（V2 家族版式，2026-10-11 起）。
 *
 * 由终审定稿的预览成品移植：hoodie-完整版.html
 * （07-开发分析与临时产物/首页v2-配图预览-2026-10-10/）。
 * 版式与文案逐字保留；页头/页脚改用共享 chrome 组件（V2Layout，其样式在页面
 * CSS 的「全局页头/页脚样式（绿色→紫罗兰映射）」段）；询盘表单保留原标记，
 * 接线换成真实投递链路（action=/api/inquiry + InquirySubmitScript）；SEO 文案
 * 取自 src/lib/seo.ts 的同路径条目，与旧版逐字一致（面包屑 / 内链 / 原文经
 * 全站终审核对）。页面级样式：src/styles/pages/hoodie-manufacturer.css。
 */
import V2Layout from "../layouts/V2Layout.astro";
import { getSeo } from "../lib/seo";
import "../styles/pages/hoodie-manufacturer.css";
---

<V2Layout seo={getSeo("/hoodie-manufacturer/")} navCurrent={["/production/", "/hoodie-manufacturer/"]}>
<header class="hero">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="hero__main">
        <nav class="breadcrumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
          <a href="/">Home</a>
          <span class="sep">/</span>
          <span class="current">Hoodie Manufacturer</span>
        </nav>
        <p class="hero__eyebrow">Custom hoodies / Private label development</p>
        <h1 class="section-h1 hero__h1">Hoodie Manufacturer for Custom and Private Label Brands</h1>
        <p class="hero__lead">Tell us what kind of hoodie you want to make. We’ll help you work through the fit, fabric, construction, artwork, labels, sample, and packing before you move into bulk production. You can start with a tech pack, a reference garment, or clear product photos</p>
        <div class="hero__actions">
          <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/contact-us/">Send your hoodie brief <span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
          <a class="hero-link" href="#styles">Explore hoodie options</a>
          <a class="hero-link" href="/contact-us/">Send an inquiry</a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="hero__media">
        <figure>
          <img src="/uploads/hoodie-manufacturer/hero-blank-pullover.webp" alt="Blank pullover hoodie on a model as a visual style reference" width="1248" height="1248" loading="eager" />
        </figure><p class="hero__cap"><b>Product direction</b>Visual reference image · final specifications require confirmation</p>
      </div>
      <div class="hero__rail">
        <div class="hero__rail-item">
          <span class="num">01</span>
          <span>Start with your references</span>
        </div>
        <div class="hero__rail-item">
          <span class="num">02</span>
          <span>Check the physical sample</span>
        </div>
        <div class="hero__rail-item">
          <span class="num">03</span>
          <span>Confirm details before bulk</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </header>

  <nav class="indexbar" aria-label="Section index">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="indexbar__list">
        <a class="indexbar__item" href="#buyers"><span class="num">01</span>Buyer fit</a>
        <a class="indexbar__item" href="#styles"><span class="num">02</span>Styles</a>
        <a class="indexbar__item" href="#specification"><span class="num">03</span>Specification</a>
        <a class="indexbar__item" href="#fabric"><span class="num">04</span>Fabric &amp; GSM</a>
        <a class="indexbar__item" href="#decoration"><span class="num">05</span>Decoration</a>
        <a class="indexbar__item" href="#labels"><span class="num">06</span>Private label</a>
        <a class="indexbar__item" href="#workflow"><span class="num">07</span>Workflow</a>
        <a class="indexbar__item" href="#quality"><span class="num">08</span>Quality</a>
        <a class="indexbar__item" href="#inquiry"><span class="num">09</span>Inquiry</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </nav>

  <section id="buyers" class="section section--paper">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="sec-head">
        <div class="sec-head__main">
          <p class="section-eyebrow"><span class="num">01</span> / Buyer requirements</p>
          <h2 class="section-h2">What Do You Need from a Hoodie Manufacturer?</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="sec-head__note">
          <p class="section-lead">You need clear answers before you spend money on a sample or bulk order. We’ll help organize the product details, and we won’t guess at MOQ, price, timing, capacity, or certification. Whether you’re reaching out to a hoodie manufacturer, a hoodie supplier, or a hoodie vendor, the same product details decide whether a quote is useful. For the full picture of how we support development, decoration, labeling, and quality, see our <a class="textlink" href="/our-services/">services overview</a></p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="m-list">
        <div class="m-list__row">
          <span class="num">01</span>
          <h3 class="section-h3 section-h3--lg">Private Label Brands</h3>
          <p>You may have a sketch, mood board, tech pack, or garment you already like. We help turn those references into clear decisions about fit, fabric, branding, and finishing</p>
        </div>
        <div class="m-list__row">
          <span class="num">02</span>
          <h3 class="section-h3 section-h3--lg">Wholesalers and Retailers</h3>
          <p>You need products that are easier to reorder and compare. Clear size, color, label, and packing records give both sides a consistent reference for future orders</p>
        </div>
        <div class="m-list__row">
          <span class="num">03</span>
          <h3 class="section-h3 section-h3--lg">Sourcing and Procurement Teams</h3>
          <p>If several people need to approve the order, one clear product record saves time. It keeps specifications, inspection points, packing, and delivery details in the same conversation</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="styles" class="section section--cream">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="sec-head">
        <div class="sec-head__main">
          <p class="section-eyebrow"><span class="num">02</span> / Hoodie styles</p>
          <h2 class="section-h2">What Kind of Hoodie Are You Planning to Make?</h2>
        </div>
        <p class="note sec-head__note">Start with the silhouette. It affects the pattern, fabric, construction, and space available for your artwork. These images are style references, not completed customer orders</p>
      </div>
      <div class="m-grid m-grid--4">
        <article class="m-card">
          <figure><img src="/uploads/hoodie-manufacturer/style-blank-pullover.webp" alt="Blank pullover hoodie on a model" width="1248" height="1248" loading="lazy" /></figure>
          <span class="num">Style 01</span>
          <h3 class="section-h3">Blank Pullover Hoodies</h3>
          <p>A practical base for printing, embroidery, and private labels. Confirm the hood, pocket, rib, fit, and fabric before you approve the sample. It’s the starting point for most pullover hoodie manufacturer comparisons</p>
        </article>
        <article class="m-card">
          <figure><img src="/uploads/hoodie-manufacturer/style-full-zip.webp" alt="Full zip-up hoodie on a model" width="1248" height="1248" loading="lazy" /></figure>
          <span class="num">Style 02</span>
          <h3 class="section-h3">Full Zip-Up Hoodies</h3>
          <p>A good option for layering, retail collections, uniforms, or bulk zip up hoodies ordered for multiple stores. Pay attention to the zipper, front alignment, pocket position, and hood finish</p>
        </article>
        <article class="m-card">
          <figure><img src="/uploads/hoodie-manufacturer/style-quarter-zip.webp" alt="Quarter-zip hoodie on a model" width="1248" height="1248" loading="lazy" /></figure>
          <span class="num">Style 03</span>
          <h3 class="section-h3">Quarter-Zip Hoodies</h3>
          <p>This style puts more focus on the collar and neckline. A reference sample helps explain the placket length, zipper, collar shape, and intended fit</p>
        </article>
        <article class="m-card">
          <figure><img src="/uploads/hoodie-manufacturer/style-oversized.webp" alt="Oversized drop-shoulder hoodie on a model" width="1248" height="1248" loading="lazy" /></figure>
          <span class="num">Style 04</span>
          <h3 class="section-h3">Oversized Hoodies</h3>
          <p>Oversized does not simply mean choosing a larger size. Shoulder drop, sleeve volume, body length, hood scale, rib, and fabric structure shape the final look. This is one of the most requested directions when buyers search for an oversized hoodie manufacturer</p>
        </article>
        <article class="m-card">
          <figure><img src="/uploads/hoodie-manufacturer/style-heavyweight.webp" alt="Heavyweight hoodie on a model" width="1248" height="1248" loading="lazy" /></figure>
          <span class="num">Style 05</span>
          <h3 class="section-h3">Heavyweight Hoodies</h3>
          <p>Choose this direction when you want a more substantial handfeel and stable shape. GSM matters, but so do the knit, surface, reverse, shrinkage, and rib recovery. Buyers comparing a heavyweight hoodie manufacturer usually start here</p>
        </article>
        <article class="m-card">
          <figure><img src="/uploads/hoodie-manufacturer/style-cropped.webp" alt="Cropped hoodie on a model" width="1248" height="1248" loading="lazy" /></figure>
          <span class="num">Style 06</span>
          <h3 class="section-h3">Cropped Hoodies</h3>
          <p>The right crop depends on the customer and size range. Check body length together with width, sleeves, hem finish, and where the garment should sit</p>
        </article>
        <article class="m-card">
          <figure><img src="/uploads/hoodie-manufacturer/style-sleeveless.webp" alt="Sleeveless hoodie on a model" width="1248" height="1248" loading="lazy" /></figure>
          <span class="num">Style 07</span>
          <h3 class="section-h3">Sleeveless Hoodies</h3>
          <p>Useful for athletic, streetwear, and layered looks. Confirm the armhole, shoulder width, edge finish, hood weight, and how the fabric hangs on the body</p>
        </article>
        <article class="m-card">
          <figure><img src="/uploads/hoodie-manufacturer/style-acid-wash.webp" alt="Acid-wash vintage hoodie on a model" width="1248" height="1248" loading="lazy" /></figure>
          <span class="num">Style 08</span>
          <h3 class="section-h3">Acid-Wash Vintage Hoodies</h3>
          <p>A washed finish changes the base color, surface, and handfeel. Confirm the wash recipe, shade tolerance, placement, and appearance after laundering</p>
        </article>
        <article class="m-card">
          <figure><img src="/uploads/hoodie-manufacturer/style-camo.webp" alt="Camouflage hoodie on a model" width="1248" height="1248" loading="lazy" /></figure>
          <span class="num">Style 09</span>
          <h3 class="section-h3">Camo Hoodies</h3>
          <p>Camouflage graphics need a clear repeat, color direction, and placement plan. Confirm how the artwork aligns across the hood, body, sleeves, and pocket</p>
        </article>
        <article class="m-card">
          <figure><img src="/uploads/hoodie-manufacturer/style-embroidered.webp" alt="Embroidered hoodie on a model" width="1248" height="1248" loading="lazy" /></figure>
          <span class="num">Style 10</span>
          <h3 class="section-h3">Embroidered Hoodies</h3>
          <p>Embroidery adds texture and structure to the garment. Review the digitized file, stitch density, backing, placement, and how the fabric supports the design</p>
        </article>
        <article class="m-card">
          <figure><img src="/uploads/hoodie-manufacturer/style-graphic-screen-print.webp" alt="Graphic screen-printed hoodie on a model" width="1248" height="1248" loading="lazy" /></figure>
          <span class="num">Style 11</span>
          <h3 class="section-h3">Graphic Screen-Printed Hoodies</h3>
          <p>Large graphics need a clear artwork file and placement reference. Confirm ink colors, print size, registration, handfeel, and wash expectations before bulk</p>
        </article>
        <article class="m-card">
          <figure><img src="/uploads/hoodie-manufacturer/style-puff-print.webp" alt="Puff-print hoodie on a model" width="1248" height="1248" loading="lazy" /></figure>
          <span class="num">Style 12</span>
          <h3 class="section-h3">Puff-Print Hoodies</h3>
          <p>Puff print creates a raised graphic surface. Check the artwork detail, expansion, edge definition, pressure, placement, and final handfeel on the selected fabric</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="specification" class="section section--paper">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="sec-head">
        <div class="sec-head__main">
          <p class="section-eyebrow"><span class="num">03</span> / Custom specification</p>
          <h2 class="section-h2">What Should You Confirm Before Sampling?</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="sec-head__note">
          <p class="section-lead">A hoodie is more than a front and back image. Confirm the measurements, fabric, construction, color, branding, and packing together. One change can affect several other details</p>
                  <a class="textlink" href="/contact-us/">Prepare a product brief</a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="m-list">
        <div class="m-list__row">
          <span class="num">01</span>
          <h3 class="section-h3 section-h3--lg">Fit, Measurements, and Grading</h3>
          <p>Tell us who will wear the hoodie and how it should fit. A size chart, approved garment, or key measurements gives the sample team a clear starting point</p>
        </div>
        <div class="m-list__row">
          <span class="num">02</span>
          <h3 class="section-h3 section-h3--lg">Hood and Drawcord Details</h3>
          <p>Choose the hood layers, panel shape, neckline, lining, drawcord, eyelets, and cord ends. Also check whether drawcords suit the age group and destination market</p>
        </div>
        <div class="m-list__row">
          <span class="num">03</span>
          <h3 class="section-h3 section-h3--lg">Pocket, Rib, Seams, and Trims</h3>
          <p>Show the pocket shape, rib depth, seam type, topstitching, reinforcement, zipper, snaps, and other hardware. Small construction details can change both appearance and cost</p>
        </div>
        <div class="m-list__row">
          <span class="num">04</span>
          <h3 class="section-h3 section-h3--lg">Color and Garment Finish</h3>
          <p>Share a color reference and the finish you want: clean solid, melange, faded, washed, or another effect. The actual process depends on the chosen fabric</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="fabric" class="section section--dark">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="sec-head">
        <div class="sec-head__main">
          <p class="section-eyebrow"><span class="num">04</span> / Fabric and GSM</p>
          <h2 class="section-h2">Which Fabric and GSM Suit Your Hoodie?</h2>
        </div>
        <p class="note sec-head__note">GSM tells you the fabric weight per square metre. It does not tell you everything about warmth, softness, shrinkage, or quality. Compare real swatches as well as the composition, knit, surface, and finish</p>
      </div>
      <div class="m-grid m-grid--3">
        <article class="m-card">
          <figure><img src="/uploads/hoodie-manufacturer/fabric-cotton.webp" alt="Cotton fabric arranged as a visual material reference" width="1248" height="1248" loading="lazy" /></figure>
          <span class="num">Material direction 01</span>
          <h3 class="section-h3">Cotton</h3>
          <p>Cotton is often chosen for a natural feel. The yarn, knit, finish, shrinkage, color, and garment construction still need to match your product</p>
        </article>
        <article class="m-card">
          <figure><img src="/uploads/hoodie-manufacturer/fabric-fleece.webp" alt="Brushed fleece fabric arranged as a visual material reference" width="1248" height="1248" loading="lazy" /></figure>
          <span class="num">Material direction 02</span>
          <h3 class="section-h3">Brushed Fleece</h3>
          <p>The brushed inside can feel warmer and softer. Check the pile, shedding, thickness, recovery, and how it works with seams, prints, and embroidery</p>
        </article>
        <article class="m-card">
          <figure><img src="/uploads/hoodie-manufacturer/fabric-french-terry.webp" alt="French terry fabric arranged as a visual material reference" width="1248" height="1248" loading="lazy" /></figure>
          <span class="num">Material direction 03</span>
          <h3 class="section-h3">French Terry</h3>
          <p>French terry has loops on the inside instead of a fully brushed surface. Compare the loop structure, weight, stability, and wash result on a real swatch</p>
        </article>
        <article class="m-card">
          <figure><img src="/uploads/hoodie-manufacturer/fabric-polyester.webp" alt="Polyester fabric arranged as a visual material reference" width="1248" height="1248" loading="lazy" /></figure>
          <span class="num">Material direction 04</span>
          <h3 class="section-h3">Polyester</h3>
          <p>Polyester may suit performance or easy-care products. Discuss stretch, surface, pilling, color method, moisture needs, and decoration before choosing the fabric</p>
        </article>
        <article class="m-card">
          <figure><img src="/uploads/hoodie-manufacturer/fabric-blend.webp" alt="Blended hoodie fabric arranged as a visual material reference" width="1248" height="1248" loading="lazy" /></figure>
          <span class="num">Material direction 05</span>
          <h3 class="section-h3">Cotton-Blend Fabrics</h3>
          <p>A blend can balance handfeel, stability, durability, and cost. The fiber ratio matters, but the knit and finishing also change how the hoodie performs</p>
        </article>
      </div>
      <div class="m-tip">
        <strong>Not sure where to start?</strong>
        <p>Tell us the climate, season, fit, handfeel, artwork, wash expectations, and price range. Those details help narrow the fabric options before you review swatches and samples</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="decoration" class="section section--cream">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="sec-head">
        <div class="sec-head__main">
          <p class="section-eyebrow"><span class="num">05</span> / Logo application</p>
          <h2 class="section-h2">How Do You Want Your Logo to Look and Feel?</h2>
        </div>
        <p class="note sec-head__note">Send vector artwork if you have it. Tell us the size, position, colors, surface effect, and wash expectations. Review a strike-off or decorated sample before bulk production. This applies whether you need a small reorder or bulk hoodies for printing across several styles. Puff print is also available — see the Puff-Print Hoodies style above for a visual reference</p>
      </div>
      <div class="m-grid m-grid--3">
        <article class="m-card">
          <figure><img src="/uploads/hoodie-manufacturer/decoration-screen-print.webp" alt="Screen printed lettering on a sweatshirt as a visual process reference" width="1248" height="1248" loading="lazy" /></figure>
          <span class="num">Process 01</span>
          <h3 class="section-h3">Screen Printing</h3>
          <p>Works well for bold spot-color graphics. Send the artwork size, colors, placement, and preferred handfeel so the print can be tested on your fabric</p>
        </article>
        <article class="m-card">
          <figure><img src="/uploads/hoodie-manufacturer/decoration-dtg.webp" alt="Direct-to-garment printed lettering as a visual process reference" width="1248" height="1248" loading="lazy" /></figure>
          <span class="num">Process 02</span>
          <h3 class="section-h3">DTG Printing</h3>
          <p>Useful for detailed or multicolor artwork on compatible garments. Check the print color, pretreatment, handfeel, and wash result before bulk approval</p>
        </article>
        <article class="m-card">
          <figure><img src="/uploads/hoodie-manufacturer/decoration-embroidery.webp" alt="Embroidered lettering on a hoodie as a visual process reference" width="1248" height="1248" loading="lazy" /></figure>
          <span class="num">Process 03</span>
          <h3 class="section-h3">Flat Embroidery</h3>
          <p>A durable choice for compact logos. Stitch count, thread, backing, edge quality, placement, and fabric stability all affect the finished result. A common choice for buyers researching an embroidered hoodie manufacturer for brand logos</p>
        </article>
        <article class="m-card">
          <figure><img src="/uploads/hoodie-manufacturer/decoration-chenille.webp" alt="Chenille embroidered lettering as a visual process reference" width="1248" height="1248" loading="lazy" /></figure>
          <span class="num">Process 04</span>
          <h3 class="section-h3">Chenille Embroidery</h3>
          <p>Creates a raised varsity-style texture. Keep the artwork bold enough for clean borders and confirm the backing, garment weight, and embroidery details</p>
        </article>
        <article class="m-card">
          <figure><img src="/uploads/hoodie-manufacturer/decoration-heat-transfer.webp" alt="Heat transfer lettering on a hoodie as a visual process reference" width="1248" height="1248" loading="lazy" /></figure>
          <span class="num">Process 05</span>
          <h3 class="section-h3">Heat Transfer</h3>
          <p>Can work for small graphics, names, numbers, or special effects. Check flexibility, edges, pressing position, finish, and wash requirements on the selected fabric</p>
        </article>
        <article class="m-card">
          <figure><img src="/uploads/hoodie-manufacturer/decoration-embossed.webp" alt="Raised tonal lettering as a visual embossed effect reference" width="1248" height="1248" loading="lazy" /></figure>
          <span class="num">Process 06</span>
          <h3 class="section-h3">Embossed or Debossed Effects</h3>
          <p>Choose this for a subtle raised or pressed effect. Feasibility depends on the fabric, backing, artwork detail, pressure method, and care expectations</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="labels" class="section section--paper">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="sec-head">
        <div class="sec-head__main">
          <p class="section-eyebrow"><span class="num">06</span> / Private labels</p>
          <h2 class="section-h2">What Should Carry Your Brand Name?</h2>
        </div>
        <p class="note sec-head__note">This is the core of working with a private label hoodie manufacturer — every brand touchpoint on the garment needs to match. Your main label, size label, hangtag, bag sticker, and carton should use the same approved information. Confirm the wording, artwork, barcode, position, and attachment before production</p>
      </div>
      <div class="m-grid m-grid--5">
        <article class="m-card">
          <figure><img src="/uploads/hoodie-manufacturer/label-woven.webp" alt="Woven neck label as a visual private label reference" width="1248" height="1248" loading="lazy" /></figure>
          <span class="num">01</span>
          <h3 class="section-h3">Woven Main Label</h3>
          <p>Send vector artwork and confirm the size, fold, colors, position, and sewing method. Review the label sample before it is used on bulk garments</p>
        </article>
        <article class="m-card">
          <figure><img src="/uploads/hoodie-manufacturer/label-size.webp" alt="Garment size label as a visual private label reference" width="1248" height="1248" loading="lazy" /></figure>
          <span class="num">02</span>
          <h3 class="section-h3">Size Label</h3>
          <p>Use the same size system on the garment, packing list, bag sticker, and carton. The label can be separate or combined with another label</p>
        </article>
        <article class="m-card">
          <figure><img src="/uploads/hoodie-manufacturer/label-heat-transfer.webp" alt="Heat transfer neck label as a visual private label reference" width="1248" height="1248" loading="lazy" /></figure>
          <span class="num">03</span>
          <h3 class="section-h3">Heat Transfer Neck Label</h3>
          <p>A printed neck label can reduce sewn-in bulk on suitable fabrics. Test the opacity, position, pressing result, comfort, and wear resistance first</p>
        </article>
        <article class="m-card">
          <figure><img src="/uploads/hoodie-manufacturer/label-pu-patch.webp" alt="PU brand patch as a visual private label reference" width="1248" height="1248" loading="lazy" /></figure>
          <span class="num">04</span>
          <h3 class="section-h3">PU or Faux-Leather Patch</h3>
          <p>Patches can sit at the hem, pocket, or sleeve. Confirm the shape, thickness, color, surface effect, edge, and attachment method</p>
        </article>
        <article class="m-card">
          <figure><img src="/uploads/hoodie-manufacturer/label-hangtag.webp" alt="Custom garment hangtag as a visual packaging reference" width="1248" height="1248" loading="lazy" /></figure>
          <span class="num">05</span>
          <h3 class="section-h3">Custom Hangtag</h3>
          <p>Provide the final approved text and barcode. Then confirm the card, size, print, finish, string or fastener, and where the tag should be attached</p>
        </article>
      </div>
      <p class="m-source">These label images are visual references. You must supply or approve the final wording, fiber content, origin, care instructions, compliance text, and barcode for your market</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="commercial" class="section section--dark">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="sec-head">
        <div class="sec-head__main">
          <p class="section-eyebrow"><span class="num">07</span> / MOQ and quotation</p>
          <h2 class="section-h2">What Affects Your MOQ and Unit Price?</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="sec-head__note">
          <p class="section-lead" style="color:var(--yv-dark-muted);">Standard orders start at 100 pieces, but there is no useful fixed answer beyond that without seeing the hoodie. Fabric, color, construction, artwork, labels, quantity, and packing can all change the MOQ and price</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="m-cols m-cols--3">
        <div class="m-col">
          <span class="num">01</span>
          <h3 class="section-h3 section-h3--lg">What Affects Your MOQ?</h3>
          <p>If you’re comparing a hoodie manufacturer with low MOQ, here’s what actually moves the number — the hoodie minimum order quantity starts at 100 pieces. The final minimum can change with fabric availability, custom color, washing, decoration, labels, trims, and your size and color split. Tell us the quantity you want to test and scale</p>
        </div>
        <div class="m-col">
          <span class="num">02</span>
          <h3 class="section-h3 section-h3--lg">What Affects Your Unit Price?</h3>
          <p>Pricing for wholesale and bulk custom hoodies depends on the same variables as a single sample order — just at scale. Price depends on fabric use, construction time, artwork, trims, labels, testing, packing, quantity, and delivery terms. A complete brief makes the quote more useful. As a planning reference, standard custom hoodies are typically quoted at <strong>US$10–15 per piece</strong>; the confirmed price follows the final specification</p>
        </div>
        <div class="m-col">
          <span class="num">03</span>
          <h3 class="section-h3 section-h3--lg">How Do You Compare Supplier Quotes?</h3>
          <p>Whether you’re comparing wholesale hoodie suppliers or individual hoodie vendors, send every supplier the same specification. Include measurements, fabric, artwork, labels, quantity, packing, destination, and trade terms so you are comparing the same product</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="workflow" class="section section--paper">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="sec-head">
        <div class="sec-head__main">
          <p class="section-eyebrow"><span class="num">08</span> / Sampling to bulk</p>
          <h2 class="section-h2">How Does Your Hoodie Move from Idea to Bulk Production?</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="sec-head__note">
          <p class="section-lead">The goal is simple: make the product clear, check a physical sample, record your approval, and use that reference when the order moves into production. This hoodie manufacturing process follows the same reviewed <a class="textlink" href="/production/">production process</a> used across all our product categories</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <ol class="m-flow">
        <li class="m-flow__step">
          <span class="num num--step">01</span>
          <h3 class="section-h3 section-h3--lg">Tell Us What You Want to Make</h3>
          <p>Share the style, quantity, target customer, references, fit, fabric, artwork, labels, packing, destination, and timing. We’ll identify what is still missing</p>
        </li>
        <li class="m-flow__step">
          <span class="num num--step">02</span>
          <h3 class="section-h3 section-h3--lg">Confirm the Product Details</h3>
          <p>Before sampling, both sides should understand the material, measurements, construction, branding, sample needs, price factors, and expected project schedule</p>
        </li>
        <li class="m-flow__step">
          <span class="num num--step">03</span>
          <h3 class="section-h3 section-h3--lg">Make and Review the Sample</h3>
          <p>Use the physical sample to check fit, fabric, sewing, trims, artwork, and labels. The sample type and timing depend on the product</p>
        </li>
        <li class="m-flow__step">
          <span class="num num--step">04</span>
          <h3 class="section-h3 section-h3--lg">Send One Clear Set of Comments</h3>
          <p>Measure and wear the sample, then mark every requested change in one file. This gives the next sample or bulk order a cleaner reference</p>
        </li>
        <li class="m-flow__step">
          <span class="num num--step">05</span>
          <h3 class="section-h3 section-h3--lg">Move into Bulk Production</h3>
          <p>Once the sample and commercial details are approved, the order can move through cutting, sewing, decoration, finishing, checks, and packing</p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="quality" class="section section--dark">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="sec-head">
        <div class="sec-head__main">
          <p class="section-eyebrow"><span class="num">09</span> / Quality checkpoints</p>
          <h2 class="section-h2">What Should Be Checked Before Your Hoodies Are Packed?</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="sec-head__note">
          <p class="section-lead" style="color:var(--yv-dark-muted);">Agree on the inspection method, tolerances, defect rules, and report before production. The checkpoints below show what to plan; they are not a fixed AQL promise for every order</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="m-list">
        <div class="m-list__row">
          <span class="num">01</span>
          <h3 class="section-h3 section-h3--lg">Check the Incoming Fabric</h3>
          <p>Compare the material with the approved reference. Check identity, color, width, weight or construction where needed, surface condition, and visible defects</p>
        </div>
        <div class="m-list__row">
          <span class="num">02</span>
          <h3 class="section-h3 section-h3--lg">Check the Hoodie While It Is Being Sewn</h3>
          <p>Look at key measurements, seams, stitching, pocket and zipper alignment, reinforcement, matching, and obvious workmanship problems during production</p>
        </div>
        <div class="m-list__row">
          <span class="num">03</span>
          <h3 class="section-h3 section-h3--lg">Check Artwork and Labels</h3>
          <p>Confirm placement, size, spelling, color, direction, and attachment against the approved files. The buyer must approve all barcode and legal label content</p>
        </div>
        <div class="m-list__row">
          <span class="num">04</span>
          <h3 class="section-h3 section-h3--lg">Check the Finished Order and Packing</h3>
          <p>Review garments, quantities, size and color assortments, labels, folding, bags, carton marks, and documents using the agreed inspection plan</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="packing" class="section section--cream">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="sec-head">
        <div class="sec-head__main">
          <p class="section-eyebrow"><span class="num">10</span> / Packaging and delivery</p>
          <h2 class="section-h2">How Should Your Hoodies Be Packed and Delivered?</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="sec-head__note">
          <p class="section-lead">Tell us what happens after the cartons leave production. Retail, e-commerce, warehouse, and wholesale orders may need different labels, bags, carton assortments, and delivery documents</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="m-list">
        <div class="m-list__row">
          <span class="num">01</span>
          <h3 class="section-h3 section-h3--lg">Pack Each Hoodie</h3>
          <p>Confirm how the hoodie should be folded, bagged, labeled, and identified. Add inserts, warning text, stickers, or barcodes only when your sales channel needs them. Custom hoodie packaging is confirmed per project, not assumed from a template</p>
        </div>
        <div class="m-list__row">
          <span class="num">02</span>
          <h3 class="section-h3 section-h3--lg">Plan the Carton Assortment</h3>
          <p>Choose solid-size or mixed-size cartons, units per carton, shipping marks, order references, destination labels, and the packing-list format</p>
        </div>
        <div class="m-list__row">
          <span class="num">03</span>
          <h3 class="section-h3 section-h3--lg">Confirm the Delivery Handoff</h3>
          <p>Share the country, delivery address or port, target date, transport preference, Incoterm, and document needs. Freight cost and timing are confirmed for each shipment</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="factory" class="section section--dark">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="sec-head">
        <div class="sec-head__main">
          <p class="section-eyebrow"><span class="num">11</span> / Production environment</p>
          <h2 class="section-h2">Where Does Each Production Step Happen?</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="sec-head__note">
          <p class="section-lead" style="color:var(--yv-dark-muted);">These photos show garment-production equipment and working areas. Use them together with live communication, current company details, project records, and any documents you need to verify before ordering. See more <a class="textlink" href="/production/#factory-record">production evidence</a> across all categories on the production overview page</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="m-grid m-grid--4">
        <article class="m-card">
          <figure><img src="/uploads/factory/factory-pattern.webp" alt="Pattern preparation and garment construction work in the supplied production environment" width="2000" height="1500" loading="lazy" /></figure>
          <span class="num">Stage 01</span>
          <h3 class="section-h3">Prepare the Pattern and Marker</h3>
          <p>Patterns define the garment pieces. Markers organize those pieces for cutting and help the team follow the approved measurements and size range</p>
        </article>
        <article class="m-card">
          <figure><img src="/uploads/factory/factory-cutting.webp" alt="Fabric cutting table and cutting equipment in the supplied production environment" width="2400" height="1600" loading="lazy" /></figure>
          <span class="num">Stage 02</span>
          <h3 class="section-h3">Cut the Fabric</h3>
          <p>Fabric is prepared and cut into the required panels. The cutting plan depends on the fabric, sizes, colors, and product construction</p>
        </article>
        <article class="m-card">
          <figure><img src="/uploads/factory/factory-sewing-line-detail.webp" alt="Garment sewing operators working at production stations" width="2400" height="1600" loading="lazy" /></figure>
          <span class="num">Stage 03</span>
          <h3 class="section-h3">Sew the Hoodie</h3>
          <p>The sewing team joins the body, sleeves, hood, pocket, rib, zipper, labels, and reinforcement points according to the approved specification</p>
        </article>
        <article class="m-card">
          <figure><img src="/uploads/factory/factory-packout.webp" alt="Finished garments being folded and prepared for packing" width="2400" height="1798" loading="lazy" /></figure>
          <span class="num">Stage 04</span>
          <h3 class="section-h3">Finish and Prepare for Packing</h3>
          <p>Finished hoodies are checked, trimmed, pressed where suitable, folded, and prepared for the packing method confirmed with the buyer</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="faq" class="section section--paper">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="sec-head">
        <div class="sec-head__main">
          <p class="section-eyebrow"><span class="num">12</span> / Buyer questions</p>
          <h2 class="section-h2">Questions Buyers Ask Before Ordering Hoodies</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="sec-head__note">
          <p class="section-lead">Start with the answers below, then confirm project-specific details in the quote, sample record, order documents, and production plan</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="faq">
        <details>
          <summary>
            <span class="num">01</span>
            <span class="q">What Should I Send for a Custom Hoodie Quote?</span>
            <span class="plus" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
          </summary>
          <div class="faq-body">Send product photos or a tech pack, your size chart, target fit, fabric or GSM preference, colors, quantity, artwork, labels, packing, destination, and target date. If something is undecided, tell us. We can separate confirmed details from open questions</div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>
            <span class="num">02</span>
            <span class="q">Can I Start Without a Complete Tech Pack?</span>
            <span class="plus" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
          </summary>
          <div class="faq-body">Yes. You can start with a reference garment, annotated photos, sketches, and key measurements. A complete tech pack is clearer, but missing information can be identified before an accurate quote or sample is prepared</div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>
            <span class="num">03</span>
            <span class="q">What Is the MOQ for Custom Hoodies?</span>
            <span class="plus" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
          </summary>
          <div class="faq-body">The hoodie minimum order quantity starts at 100 pieces, and the confirmed minimum depends on fabric, color, washing, construction, artwork, labels, trims, and the size and color split. Send the quantity you want to test and your expected reorder quantity</div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>
            <span class="num">04</span>
            <span class="q">How Long Do Samples and Bulk Production Take?</span>
            <span class="plus" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
          </summary>
          <div class="faq-body">The schedule depends on the style, material, decoration, labels, sample type, revisions, quantity, packing, and season. Once these details are clear, the project can be given a more useful timeline</div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>
            <span class="num">05</span>
            <span class="q">How Do We Keep Bulk Production Close to the Approved Sample?</span>
            <span class="plus" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
          </summary>
          <div class="faq-body">Use the approved sample and written specification as the shared reference. Record measurements, materials, artwork, labels, approved changes, tolerances, and the inspection method before bulk production begins</div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>
            <span class="num">06</span>
            <span class="q">Which GSM Should I Choose for a Hoodie?</span>
            <span class="plus" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
          </summary>
          <div class="faq-body">Start with the climate, season, fit, layering use, price range, and handfeel you want. Then compare real swatches. GSM matters, but composition, knit, brushing, and finishing also change the result</div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>
            <span class="num">07</span>
            <span class="q">Can I Add Custom Labels, Hangtags, and Packaging?</span>
            <span class="plus" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
          </summary>
          <div class="faq-body">These items — including custom hoodie packaging — can be included in the product brief. Send final artwork, wording, dimensions, placement, barcode data, and packing instructions. Feasibility and minimums are confirmed for each item</div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>
            <span class="num">08</span>
            <span class="q">How Do I Start a Hoodie Manufacturing Inquiry?</span>
            <span class="plus" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
          </summary>
          <div class="faq-body">Send one focused product brief through the contact page. Include the style, quantity, sizes, colors, references, fabric, branding, destination, and target date. That is enough to begin identifying the next decisions</div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

